-- Listening Lesson 14 step by step (IELTS-14 v0.3.0), steps 4-6 of 6: five guided steps and one mixed check, in the Lesson 6 format
-- (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer, the transcript only after submitting). Audio is cut
-- from the owner-approved lesson recording (no new speech) and stored in the private ielts-targeted-listening bucket
-- by exact SHA-256. Owner instruction 2026-10-28 15:17 (Asia/Bishkek): 'Go next'. Named-student pilot with
-- requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_LISTENING_06.json.
do $steps$
declare tF constant text := 'PROFESSOR GRANT: Come in, Amira, Josh. So, how''s the presentation coming along?
JOSH: Getting there, I think. The survey was harder than we expected, though.
PROFESSOR GRANT: In what way?
AMIRA: Well, we were worried at first that we wouldn''t get enough responses, but in the end thirty-two businesses replied, which was more than we needed.
JOSH: The real problem was the wording. Several owners thought ''engagement'' meant how many followers they had, so their answers to question four don''t really tell us anything.
AMIRA: And we sent it out too late. Most replies came in last week, so we''ve had very little time to analyse them.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Those are both useful things to mention when you discuss the limitations. Now, let''s go through the structure. You''ve got a background section first?
AMIRA: Yes, but it''s over four minutes long at the moment.
PROFESSOR GRANT: That''s far too much for a fifteen-minute talk. I''d cut it to about one minute. The audience will know the basics.
JOSH: OK, we''ll shorten it. Then we have the survey results.
PROFESSOR GRANT: What''s the most surprising finding?
AMIRA: That the smallest businesses, the ones with only one or two staff, post more often than the larger ones.
PROFESSOR GRANT: That''s a strong hook. Why not open with it, rather than burying it in the middle?
JOSH: Good idea. So the results go at the start.
AMIRA: What about the slide on the history of social media? I spent quite a long time on that.
PROFESSOR GRANT: I can see it was a lot of work, but it doesn''t connect to your question. I''d leave it out altogether.
AMIRA: Fair enough.
JOSH: And the recommendations at the end? We were going to leave them as they are.
PROFESSOR GRANT: They''re sensible, but rather general. Each one would be more convincing with a real example from one of the businesses you surveyed.
JOSH: Right, we can do that.
AMIRA: One more thing about the results. We''ve used pie charts for everything at the moment.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Pie charts work well for showing what share of your sample each size of business made up. But for how often each group posts, you''re asking the audience to compare amounts across groups, and that''s much easier to see as bars side by side.
JOSH: So should we just turn everything into bar charts?
PROFESSOR GRANT: No, the pie chart is the right choice for business size.
AMIRA: Or we could leave the pie charts as they are and add the exact figures to each slice.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Figures help, but the audience would still be comparing slices from different pies. It''s only the posting chart that needs to change.
JOSH: So we keep the pie chart for business size and change the posting one to a bar chart.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Exactly. And how long will you spend on the limitations you mentioned?
JOSH: Honestly, I thought we''d skip them. We''re short of time as it is.
AMIRA: But they''re the reason question four doesn''t tell us much. If we don''t say so, someone will ask. Could they go at the very end, after the recommendations?
PROFESSOR GRANT: I''d put them just before the recommendations, so that each recommendation can show how you''d deal with a limitation. It doesn''t need long: thirty seconds would show you''ve thought critically about your own data.
JOSH: Fine, a short slide on limitations, then, just before the recommendations. By the way, I heard the deadline had been moved to next Friday.
PROFESSOR GRANT: That''s for the marketing group, I''m afraid. Yours is still this Thursday, as planned.
JOSH: Ah. OK, Thursday it is.
PROFESSOR GRANT: And who''s presenting which part?
JOSH: I was going to do the results and the recommendations, and Amira the background.
AMIRA: Actually, since the background is now so short, wouldn''t it make more sense for me to do the recommendations as well? I interviewed most of the owners, so I know the examples.
JOSH: That makes sense. You take the recommendations, then.
PROFESSOR GRANT: Good. Send me your slides by Wednesday morning if you''d like comments.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,audio_path,audio_sha256,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-listening-l24-v1','0.1.0','Listening Lesson 14 step 4 of 6: Choose TWO','listening','guided_practice','targeted-listening-06/6c9f96f11978a10f69917b35371920151ed996204ae76a75fcc26747aeb8682b/BH-TL06-L14F_v1.mp3','6c9f96f11978a10f69917b35371920151ed996204ae76a75fcc26747aeb8682b','You will hear two students, Amira and Josh, talking to their tutor, Professor Grant, about a presentation. Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then listen to the whole discussion. Each question asks you to choose TWO things: choose the letter of the correct pair of numbers. You may pause or replay.','In ''choose TWO'' questions, three options are mentioned but rejected, changed or belong to someone else. Tick an option only when it is confirmed.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Which TWO problems with the survey do the students mention? 1 too few replies  2 a misunderstood word  3 sending it too late  4 lost results  5 a high cost", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "59fbf038-1ceb-48b0-b905-b7f78a8076e3", "supporting_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  1 and 2"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  2 and 3"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  1 and 3"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  4 and 5"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Which TWO changes does the tutor suggest for the start of the talk? 1 cut the background  2 open with the results  3 add the history slide  4 introduce the limitations  5 show the recommendations first", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "59fbf038-1ceb-48b0-b905-b7f78a8076e3", "supporting_node_id": "4bdeaffb-7390-4b00-9955-69b9da537007", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  2 and 4"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  3 and 5"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  1 and 2"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  1 and 3"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Which TWO chart ideas does the tutor reject? 1 turn every chart into a bar chart  2 add exact figures to the pie slices  3 a bar chart for posting  4 a pie chart for business size  5 a line chart", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "59fbf038-1ceb-48b0-b905-b7f78a8076e3", "supporting_node_id": "4bdeaffb-7390-4b00-9955-69b9da537007", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  1 and 3"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  1 and 2"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  2 and 4"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  3 and 4"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Which TWO parts will Amira present? 1 the background  2 the results  3 the limitations  4 the recommendations  5 the history of social media", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "5e1150ab-ce8c-4037-88cd-0c89cb6632fb", "supporting_node_id": "59fbf038-1ceb-48b0-b905-b7f78a8076e3", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  2 and 4"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  1 and 2"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  3 and 5"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  1 and 4"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Which TWO deadlines or times are mentioned for THESE students? 1 this Thursday  2 next Friday  3 Wednesday morning  4 next Monday  5 last week", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "59fbf038-1ceb-48b0-b905-b7f78a8076e3", "supporting_node_id": "19e4299c-70f4-4d30-8726-4416be272056", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  1 and 3"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  1 and 2"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  2 and 3"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  4 and 5"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Which TWO things will the students do to the recommendations? 1 add real examples  2 leave them unchanged  3 cut them  4 link each one to a limitation  5 move them to the start", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "59fbf038-1ceb-48b0-b905-b7f78a8076e3", "supporting_node_id": "472c05b5-8cc0-43b8-b300-ae5b769d7769", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  3 and 4"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  1 and 2"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  2 and 5"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  1 and 4"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS listening exercise; not a full paper or an official IELTS recording.", "suggested_minutes": 9, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-listening-targeted-focus-v1", "passage": "LEARN FIRST: five options, two answers\n\nIn IELTS ''Choose TWO letters'' questions, you usually HEAR all five options. Three are traps:\n- mentioned but rejected (''No, ...'', ''I''d leave it out'')\n- true for someone else (''That''s for the marketing group'')\n- an old plan that changes (''I was going to ... Actually ...'')\nTick an option only when it is confirmed. Here the five options are numbered 1-5, and each answer choice A-D shows a pair of numbers.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE\nWhich TWO will be in the final talk? 1 a pie chart for business size  2 a pie chart for posting  3 a bar chart for posting\nYou hear: ''So we keep the pie chart for business size and change the posting one to a bar chart.'' -> 1 and 3\n\nNow listen and answer six questions.", "focus": "Select relevant spoken information: identify two confirmed points when several options are mentioned and rejected."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('teacher_notes', '1 B, 2 and 3: "Several owners thought ''engagement'' meant how many followers they had" and "we sent it out too late". 1 is rejected: "more than we needed".
2 C, 1 and 2: "I''d cut it to about one minute" and "Why not open with it, rather than burying it in the middle?"
3 B, 1 and 2: to Josh, "No, the pie chart is the right choice for business size"; to Amira, "Figures help, but the audience would still be comparing slices from different pies."
4 D, 1 and 4: Josh planned "Amira the background"; then "You take the recommendations, then." Josh presents the results.
5 A, 1 and 3: "Yours is still this Thursday" and "Send me your slides by Wednesday morning". Next Friday is for the marketing group.
6 D, 1 and 4: "Each one would be more convincing with a real example" and the limitations go first "so that each recommendation can show how you''d deal with a limitation".
Read each explanation, then listen to that part again and hear the proof yourself.

